\title{Interactive Proofs, Arguments, and IOPs}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    The chain of definitions that everything else is stated in terms of, ending at the interactive oracle reduction that [ArkLib](dhsorens-0040) takes as primitive. \em{Proof} does different work here than it does in [proposition and proof](dhsorens-001F): a SNARK convinces a verifier with high probability under stated assumptions, and is not a derivation.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{Interactive proof, argument (prover computationally bounded), PCP, IOP, oracle reduction — what each relaxation buys.}
        \li{Where \em{succinct} and \em{of knowledge} enter, and what each costs the construction.}
        \li{Public-coin versus private-coin, and why every deployed system is public-coin.}
        \li{[The knowledge extractor](dhsorens-004J): what it is handed, what it must produce, and why rewinding is a problem in the non-interactive setting.}
        \li{Zero-knowledge — honest-verifier versus malicious, and how much of it a zkVM actually needs. Mostly these systems are bought for succinctness.}
    }
}

\p{
    Reading: \code{ArkLib/OracleReduction/Basic.lean} as the definitional reference; Thaler, \em{Proofs, Arguments, and Zero-Knowledge}, for the textbook chain.
}
